68:This natural mixture-of-experts approach fails on TSFMs that use instance normalization once any backbone layers are unfrozen (Figure~\ref{fig:problem_overview}b). When the standard MoE adapter recipe~\citep{wang2022adamix} is combined with load balancing~\citep{fedus2022switch}, routing on hidden states, and applied to MOMENT with RevIN, routing entropy drops to zero in most unfrozen configurations (Table~\ref{tab:adamix}). The router learns to send every window to the same expert, and the remaining experts are never used. The mechanism is what we call \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}: RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin} strips the per-window mean and variance that the router needs to distinguish input regimes (formalized in Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}; signal-ratio diagnostic in Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}), leaving it with homogenized hidden states. Even with the backbone frozen, hidden-state routing still underperforms; with the backbone unfrozen, the dominant expert receives larger gradients which reshape the backbone to serve it even more, closing a co-adaptation feedback loop within tens of training steps (Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}).
481:In contrast, \textbf{RR-MoA} maintains healthy routing entropy ($1.0$--$1.57$) and wins \textbf{54/54} configurations, with MSE improvements of $26\%$ to $79\%$ over the best fixed adapter. Notably, on Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, where the stripped statistics carry little information), RR-MoA does not improve over fixed adapters, consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction that low $R$ implies little benefit (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}).
572:In the corner case where routing signal lives entirely in the stripped statistics ($E$ deterministic in $(M,\Sigma)$, $(M,\Sigma)\perp\!\!\!\perp S$; Part~(iii), Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}), $I(X;E){-}I(S;E)=H(E)$: RevIN destroys the entire routing distribution. We use the \emph{signal ratio} $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M)+\mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)]/\overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ as a tractable monotone proxy. The bound is empirically tight (CCA $\varepsilon\in[0.09,1.26]$ nats, KSG-validated~\citep{kraskov2004ksg}; gap $0.075$ nats, Appendix~\ref{app:mi_tightness}); learnable $\alpha$ converges to $\alpha{<}0.5$ on $30/30$ runs (Appendix~\ref{app:learnable_alpha}). $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts raw-routing benefit at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}), whereas $H(E\mid S)$ alone does not ($\rho{=}{-}0.12$). Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, $\Delta{=}{+}2.9\%$) is correctly flagged a boundary case; Solar ($R{=}0.06$) is the one outlier (SSR $+43\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}).
652:\label{fig:signal_ratio}
662:MoE adapters on instance-normalized TSFMs fail because the normalization needed for distribution shift also strips the per-window statistics the router needs; raw pre-normalization routing fixes it, motivates Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}, and $R(\mathcal{D})$ identifies where it applies. The broader pattern: when a foundation model's input pipeline destroys signal a downstream router needs, the fix is restoring the input, not retuning the optimizer (replicated in a vision control, Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}). \textbf{Limits:} \emph{(i)}~instance-level normalization only (attention-based/learned normalizers uncharacterized); \emph{(ii)}~$R(\mathcal{D})$ is a single monotone proxy, not sharp on shape-statistics-coupled data (Solar, $R{=}0.06$); \emph{(iii)}~forecasting and imputation only.\label{page:end_main}
1140:Instance normalization is a \emph{deterministic lossy compression}: it maps $X \mapsto S$, discarding $(M, \Sigma)$. In rate-distortion theory~\citep{cover2006elements}, this compression has rate $R_\text{strip} = I(X; M, \Sigma \mid S)$ and distortion (for routing) $D_\text{route} = H(E \mid S) - H(E \mid X) = I(M, \Sigma; E \mid S)$, which is exactly Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(i). Under Gaussianity, the signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D})$ is proportional to the rate-distortion slope at the normalization operating point: it measures how much routing capacity is consumed by the statistics that normalization strips. Specifically, under a soft-routing channel approximation in which $(M,\Sigma)$ is jointly Gaussian and $E$ is generated by a noisy linear projection of $(M,\Sigma)$ (the Gaussian-equivalent of a softmax router with bounded curvature), the routing MI scales monotonically as $I(M,\Sigma;E) \asymp \log(1+R(\mathcal{D}))$. Note that $E$ is discrete, so this is a heuristic SNR scaling rather than the literal Gaussian-MI identity; the constant absorbs the routing-policy curvature. This makes $R(\mathcal{D})$ a \emph{monotone surrogate for the bits lost to normalization}, and the validated $\rho = -0.88$ correlation (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}) is empirical evidence \emph{for} this scaling rather than a direct corollary of the Gaussian-MI formula.
2041:\caption{\textbf{Extension to Exchange and Solar datasets} (MOMENT-small, strictly frozen, Top-2 sparse, H=96, 5 seeds for RR-MoA/AdaMix, 3 seeds for DLinear/AdaMix-last4). Exchange ($R{=}2.17$, highest signal ratio) confirms the theory prediction: $-66.5\%$ improvement. Solar ($R{=}0.06$, lowest) is an outlier: RR-MoA still achieves $-32.9\%$ improvement, indicating the Conv1d router exploits temporal shape patterns beyond location-scale (consistent with the SSR ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}). AdaMix collapses to $0.000$ entropy under last-4 unfreezing on both datasets.}

